\f1\fs22 Chip Stockwell stood in the middle of his new townhouse, surrounded by boxes he hadn\'92t yet unpacked. The place was immaculate\'97a spacious three-bedroom two-storey townhouse with minimalist decor and high-end finishes that made his previous Fridge Force quarters look like a server rack with a shower. Morning light streamed through floor-to-ceiling windows overlooking Sublime Point\'92s manicured residential circuit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Home sweet home,\'94 he muttered to no one in the kitchen, running his fingers along the quartz countertop.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried to avert his gaze from the door to the stasis closet. He\'92d never lived with one before, always preferring an old-fashioned chilling fridge. Maybe he could get this one decommissioned and turned into\'85 
\f2\i I dunno, a wine cellar
\f1\i0 , he thought. He shook his head; he\'92d never been interested in wine, never finding anything that was compatible with his fast food diet. Mostly because of the glasses, he supposed. He ate most meals straight from the paper bags and wrappers, and had no use for cutlery, so how the tax would he handle a wine glass? He was sure he would continually tip it over or splinter its fine stem in his bear-sized hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>How would he order fast food now? Would anyone deliver to Eloise\'92s compound?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Within hours of unpacking his essentials, he\'92d found the screen he\'92d used to divide his one-room living area into dining and TV spaces. His new home had separate TV, dining, and living rooms, so he leant the partially folded screen against the stasis locker door. Out of sight, but never out of mind.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Project Kebab administrative offices occupied the lowest sub-level of the building that was the farthest away from everything: the residential circuit, the main house, the administration buildings and even the spectacular views over Jamison Valley. Chip descended in an elevator that required both biometric verification and a keycard. The doors opened onto a modest lounge area, the seating furniture of which Chip could see would lack the sturdiness for his comfort. There was a lonely water cooler against the far wall and Chip wondered what vintage the water in its large inverted blue bottle would be. There was no evidence of disposable cups.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To the left was a pair of doors marked simply \'94K-LAB.\'94 As Chip approached, the doors slid open. Beyond, he saw an expansive laboratory space with workstations arranged in concentric circles around a central raised glassed-in platform. Thankfully, no sign of a Gyges Disc anywhere.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The famous Chip Stockwell,\'94 came a voice from his left. \'93I\'92ve heard so little about you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A man in his late thirties with an immaculate suit and perfectly styled hair approached, hand extended. His smile didn\'92t reach his eyes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You must be The Johnny,\'94 Chip replied (using the moniker Sarah had suggested) and accepted the handshake with his own, clammy baseball mitt-sized claw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just Johnny will do,\'94 the man said, grip firm and deliberate. \'93Or Mr Stoat. Unless you\'92re one of my life-documenters, in which case I\'92m your worst nightmare.\'94 He laughed, as if this was a joke they were both in on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m just a stasis engineer,\'94 Chip grumbled, and he did sound something like a bear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94There\'92s no \'91just\'92 anything at Project Kebab, Stockwell. Everyone here has a purpose. Some of us know what that purpose is.\'94 Johnny stood at an angle, opening up the passage between desks. \'93Come into my office,\'94 he invited. \'93Unless you would like to see the Kebabs first.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The what?\'94 Chip wondered if The Johnny knew how to smuggle Lebanese take-away into the facility.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You know,\'94 Johnny whispered. \'94The Gy\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, tax no,\'94 Chip blurted, then covered with, \'93Plenty of time for that later.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In his office, Johnny offered Chip a seat. \'94How much do you know about what we\'92re doing here?\'94 He asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nothing yet,\'94 Chip confessed, and sensed that he would prefer to keep it that way.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny\'92s smile widened fractionally. \'93We\'92re researching applications for the Kebabs\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip\'92s eyes went as wide as teacup saucers. \'94What applications?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny chuckled. \'93Well you must admit, your Fridge Force has been keeping a great secret from the wider scientific community for decades. Applying these things to the most mediocre of uses. What? Keeping milk from going sour?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip was silent, so Johnny kept blathering. \'93I mean, did you guys even consider the possibilities for energy generation? I mean, these Kebabs could be the greatest thing since nuclear.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip gagged. \'94Excuse me,\'94 he spluttered. \'93I think I ate some bad\'85\'94 He hefted his great weight off the chair and lumbered out of the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny knew Chip wasn\'92t up for this project. After Chip returned to his office with a little more colour in his face, Johnny led Chip to another office off the main laboratory.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah,\'94 Johnny announced, \'93your new pet has arrived.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked up, fixing Chip with an analytical gaze that made him feel like a specimen under a microscope.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ignore Johnny, Mr Stockwell. I\'92m Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grunted. Sarah gestured him to a chair. As he took his seat, Chip noticed three elderly individuals sitting silently in the corner of the room. Their presence was so unobtrusive that he\'92d completely missed them upon entering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let me introduce the Sages,\'94 Sarah said, following his gaze. \'94They provide\'85 alternative perspectives on our work.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The three nodded in unison, but didn\'92t speak.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alternative perspectives?\'94 Chip asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let\'92s just say they approach Kebab tech from a more philosophical angle. Some might call it mystical, but I prefer to think of it as intuitive science.\'94 Sarah pulled up a holographic display. \'94Let me show you what we\'92re dealing with.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The display showed a Gyges Disc from multiple angles, along with cascading data streams that Chip couldn\'92t fully parse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Kebabs exhibit properties that defy our current understanding of physics,\'94 Sarah explained. \'94They create localised chronological variances\'97essentially micro-pockets of altered time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fascinating,\'94 Chip uttered without commitment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is it fascinating?\'94 Sarah asked. \'93To you, Chip, is it fascinating?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip shrugged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s how stasis works, isn\'92t it? It literally freezes things, not by temperature but in time. Kebab tech preserves things by keeping them in their own bubble in time. In stasis.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s impossible,\'94 Chip said automatically.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, I don\'92t think it is impossible,\'94 Johnny stated flatly. \'93And neither does anyone else in this room.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo sat forward between the other Sages and said, \'94I\'92m twenty-six.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip regarded the wizened old husk of a man and said, \'93That\'92s nice. I hope you lived a full life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Twenty-six. Hundred. Years. Old,\'94 Solo schooled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip blinked away the absurdity of Solo\'92s claim, and cast his eye over the others in the room. He said to Johnny, \'93I don\'92t care what Goldilocks and the three fossils think. It\'92s impossible.\'94 He heaved his weight off the chair. \'93This was a mistake. I\'92m leaving.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can\'92t leave,\'94 Sarah stated flatly. \'94You know this is too important to leave in anyone else\'92s hands. We need you. We need you to assess the risk of our research and tell us when we\'92re going too far.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still standing and trying to ignore the complaints of his feet, Chip answered, \'93That\'92s easy. You\'92re already going too far.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That cannot be true, young Chip,\'94 Kleoboulos stated gently. \'93Your own Fridge Force has been harnessing and monitoring these instruments for many a long year.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And we\'92re all terrified of when something catastrophic will happen with them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which is why we need you,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Saturday arrived, Chip had settled into a routine of sorts. Mornings and afternoons were spent at the lab, evenings were devoted to unpacking and setting up his townhouse, and nights were plagued by dreams of Fridge Force agents breaking down his door. The most frightening part of those nightmares was that none of the arresting officers seemed to know his name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d installed three additional locks and a private security system that wasn\'92t connected to Sublime Point\'92s network which, unbeknown to him, Eloise had already neutralised. She also knew that the generous signing bonus she\'92d given Chip had already been split between multiple anonymous accounts, ready for Chip\'92s quick escape if necessary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Contrary to his fears, this job stressed him no more than his Fridge Force gig. While expanding stasis tech beyond its currently mild applications, he realised he\'92d always lived with the expectation that sooner or later, it would blow up in his face. Now that he had a clearer idea of how that might actually happen, he was somehow more at peace with it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Still, if there was one thing he was more stressed about, it was the family living in the townhouse next door. The Chuglinghams, the family he had not long ago been instrumental in sending to offshore detention. He felt no guilt for it, only mounting frustration at how happy they were. While they had been avoiding him assiduously, it was evident by the sounds of activities that came from their yard and through their open windows, they were getting on with their lives while he, essentially, didn\'92t have one. They were happy and chirpy and it annoyed the tax out of him. The kids were worse. They were chirpy and loud. Chip had no idea that laughter could sound so threatening.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While he breakfasted on a burrito (Eloise had personally hired a Fast Food Delivery Consultant for him), Chip checked his security system (which he still believed was functioning) on his encrypted tablet (which Eloise had easily jailbroken). On the dark web portal he\'92d set up for monitoring Fridge Force communications he found what he feared\'97an internal bulletin listing him as a \'94person of interest\'94 in an investigation of technological espionage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His tablet pinged with a message from an unknown sender:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They\'92re watching. We\'92re watching too. Sleep well.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stared at the message for a long moment before deleting it. He walked to the kitchen and faced the stasis unit he\'92d been avoiding. With a deep breath, he removed the decorative screen and opened the unit\'92s maintenance panel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If Fridge Force was coming for him, he\'92d be ready. And if Eloise Shakti thought she owned him now, she\'92d learn that Chip Stockwell had picked up more than just technical knowledge during his years in Fridge Enforcement.
